<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stone Age</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stone Age was a broad prehistoric period during which stone was widely used to make tools with an edge, a point or a percussion surface. It lasted for roughly 3.4 million years, over 99% of human history, and ended between 4000 BCE and 2000 BCE with the melting and smelting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>; in Western Asia bronze became widespread by about 3000 BCE. It is the first period of the three-age system, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and is commonly divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, when people began to leave hunting and gathering for settled life in towns and villages as agriculture spread.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The oldest indirect evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> use is fossilised animal bones with tool marks, 3.4 million years old, found in the Lower Awash Valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The oldest stone tools were excavated at Lomekwi 3 in West Turkana, northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, and are 3.3 million years old; Kenyanthropus platyops, a Pliocene hominin fossil found at Lake Turkana in 1999, may have been the earliest tool-user known.</p><p>The Stone Age is regarded as ending with innovation in smelting ore, which marks the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Between the two came the Copper Age, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> or Eneolithic, when people could smelt copper but did not yet make bronze. For much of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> the transition took place between 6000 and 2500 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> were made from flint and chert, shaped for cutting tools and weapons, and from basalt and sandstone, used for ground stone tools such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quern-stone">quern-stones</a>. Wood, bone, shell and antler were also widely used, and in the most recent part of the period clay was made into pottery. Homo habilis is believed to have built the first man-made structure, a simple arrangement of stones to hold branches in position, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> around 2 million years ago.</p><p>Cave and rock art survives from the Palaeolithic: Altamira in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was painted between 14,000 and 12,000 BCE, and the hall of bulls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lascaux">Lascaux</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> dates from about 15,000 to 10,000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1859 Jens Jacob Worsaae first proposed dividing the Stone Age into older and younger parts, from his work on Danish kitchen middens that began in 1851, and in 1865 John Lubbock coined the word Palaeolithic. In the 1920s South African archaeologists found that their collections did not fit the three-age system, and proposed a three-stage system for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in which the Early, Middle and Later Stone Age are distinct from the Palaeolithic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The names Stone Age, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> are not meant to suggest that prehistory is measured only by tool material rather than by social organisation, food sources, climate, agriculture or religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> serve as diagnostics of date rather than as a description of a people. Lithic analysis measures tools to determine their type, function and technology, experimental archaeologists make replicas to see how they were made, and flintknappers are craftsmen who reduce flint to tools.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stone Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlements and rock carvings from about 10,000 BCE in Lapland and Finnmark, once called the Komsa culture, have not been shown to be Sámi, and older theories linking <b>Stone Age</b> cultures to them have been dismissed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Outsiders have often romanticised San belief: Laurens van der Post called the Bleek collections “a sort of <b>Stone Age</b> Bible”, and his critics said that his books cast the San as “children of Nature” or “mystical ecologists”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
